# Line-ending policy — this repo is worked on from both Linux and Windows.
#
# Without this file each platform guesses (via core.autocrlf), and the guesses
# disagree: a Windows checkout writes CRLF to disk while the history holds LF,
# so the same clone reads "clean" on Windows and "121 files modified" on Linux.
# Commit from the wrong side and every file flips, turning diffs, reviews and
# worktree merges into whole-file noise.
#
# So: normalize everything to LF, in history AND on disk, on every platform.
# Python, git, VS Code and the Discord bot all handle LF fine on Windows.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Exceptions — these are interpreted by Windows itself and want CRLF on disk.
# cmd.exe mis-parses multi-line constructs (labels, goto, for) in LF batch
# files, and cscript is likewise happier with CRLF.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
*.vbs text eol=crlf

# Binary — belt and braces over text=auto's own detection, so an image can
# never be mangled by an eol rewrite.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.webp binary
*.bmp  binary
*.ico  binary
*.pdf  binary
*.zip  binary
*.jsonl -text
